{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Youths</b> — like the blind and the lame, who are not fit to stand before a king; therefore it is written: 'a blind or lame man shall not enter the house' (II Samuel 5:8). And the meaning: the house of David's kingdom." "<b>And goodly of appearance</b> — that they were not greenish and did not look like the sick." "<b>And skilled in all wisdom</b> — a general inclusive of all." "<b>And knowing knowledge</b> — that they would know, at the beginning of the speaker's discourse, what he had in his mind to speak at the end, for there are many wise men who do not know this until the speaker's discourse is finished." "<b>And understanding of discernment</b> — that they can understand from others what is in their heart and in their mind, as in: 'also in your mind do not curse the king' (Ecclesiastes 10:20). For there are wise men who labor to bring forth on their tongue what is in their heart, except by a roundabout way and in crooked paths." "<b>And in whom is strength</b> — that their constitution is strong to suppress a sneeze, and they stand before him constantly, and they can endure when needed. Now he commanded to seek one in whom these qualities are found, and they are not found in a child less than fifteen years old. And there is no need to teach them the three years, but only to write Chaldean and their language, to speak with the king in his language. Now great men went out from Jerusalem, and Daniel went out at age fifteen when Jehoiakim went to Babylon, and he reigned eight years, and then twenty-three. Some say: Zedekiah, and then four and thirty and seventy years, and he is very old in the days of Esther. Perhaps he had already died and did not go up to the Second Temple, and he is not Sheshbazzar, as is proven by complete proofs."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {5 "The comment switches rapidly between several chronological calculations and arguments whose logical connections are heavily compressed; translated hyper-literally without expansion. The phrase 'והנה כ\"ג' (and then twenty-three) is unclear in context; translated literally. The clause 'כי אם כתב כשדים' ('but only to write Chaldean') may be textually difficult, as one might expect 'ללמד' (to teach); rendered as found."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:54.938497603Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}